/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354557 #edb/ref 1069 "q-87236" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354557 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354557 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve for $k$ given that the following function is a probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{k}{{x}^{3}},\\, & 3<x<6, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354557 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7c5dab49-c074-55ae-82a5-34331ee5b508" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354557 #edb/ref 1074 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nFor the given function, the second condition states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{3}^{6}\\frac{k}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{6}\\frac{k}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{3}^{6}k{x}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{k}{2{x}^{2}}]}_{3}^{6} \\\\  & =-\\frac{k}{72}+\\frac{k}{18} \\\\  & =\\frac{k}{24}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{k}{24}=1\\,⟹\\,k=24.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186358011 #edb/ref 1080 "I and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186358011 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186358011 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1e019c0c-c668-5ed1-a867-43f40e187002" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186359039 #edb/ref 1080 "III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186359039 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186359039 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "93241e3f-6a8f-5ad8-bfac-2641dcb5f85f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186359224 #edb/ref 1080 "I and II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186359224 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186359224 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ab1ff7cd-a746-57e7-b6af-1dcad05500df" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186359373 #